\description{
Visualize persistence homologies with barcode diagrams.
}
\details{
\emph{Barcode diagrams} represent persistence data with \href{https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_segment.html}{line segments}.

They can be understood as the "persistence analogue of a Betti number",
visualizing persistence homologies in such a way that significant topological features are made evident
even in the presence of noise (Ghrist, 2008).
}
\section{Persistence data}{


Persistence data encode the values of an underlying parameter
\eqn{\epsilon} at which topological features appear ("birth") and disappear
("death"). The difference between the birth and the death of a feature is
called its "persistence". As topological features may be of different
dimensions, persistence data sets usually also include the dimension of
each feature.

Persistence data can be specified in two ways in \strong{ggtda}:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{The \code{birth} and \code{death} aesthetics}. Pre-computed persistence homologies
can be supplied directly to layer functions in \strong{ggtda}, the columns of \code{data}
with the birth and death values for each feature must be mapped to the
\code{birth} and \code{death} aesthetics, respectively.
\item \strong{The \code{dataset} aesthetic}. Instead of a pre-computed persistence homology,
\strong{ggtda} will compute the persistence homologies for arbitrary data sets.
This requires the use of a nested \code{tibble} with list columns, the \code{data}
provided to either \code{ggplot()} or the layer function should be a \code{tibble}
with a column whose entries are data sets in a format that one of the
\strong{ggtda} engines supports. This list column must be mapped to the \code{dataset} aesthetic.
}

See the examples included below or
\code{vignette("grouped-list-data", package = "ggtda")} for an extended
discussion of the \code{dataset} aesthetic's use.
}

\section{Aesthetics}{

\code{stat_barcode()} understands the following aesthetics (required aesthetics are in bold):
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{dataset} \emph{or} \code{birth}}
\item \strong{\code{dataset} \emph{or} \code{death}}
\item \code{group}
}
\code{geom_barcode()} understands the following aesthetics (required aesthetics are in bold):
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{x}}
\item \strong{\code{y}}
\item \strong{\code{xend} \emph{or} \code{yend}}
